// The forward-pass guest<->host surface (submits ETA container bytes). The WIT
// surface family is named `forward*`; ETA remains the name of the container
// byte format it ships.
//
// FOUR INTERFACES, ONE PER STATE SEMANTICS (forward_refactor.md D1):
//
//   `forward`            paged, per-token, reversibly discardable KV only
//   `forward-recurrent`  irreversibly folded recurrent state only
//   `forward-hybrid`     both, in the same forward
//   `forward-diffusion`  paged KV plus a canvas the model denoises in place
//
// THIS FILE: a block-diffusion text (DiffusionGemma). One trunk, two
// attention modes, and the guest chooses per pass which one a fire runs:
//
//   `encode`   the causal reading. Its rows attend causally over the prefix
//              and themselves and its KV writes ARE the sequence — a prompt
//              prefill, or the pass that commits a finished canvas.
//   `denoise`  the bidirectional reading. Its rows are one canvas; every row
//              attends every key of `[prefix | canvas]`, the keys after it
//              included. The KV it writes is the canvas's scratch, rewritten
//              on every step and finally overwritten by the encode pass that
//              commits the block; the prefix is read and never written.
//
// Why a fourth interface and not a verb on `forward`: `forward` promises
// per-token, reversibly discardable KV whose every fire is causal, and the
// attention-only algorithms written against it (H2O, SnapKV, TOVA, Quest)
// assume exactly that. A canvas fire breaks the assumption — its KV is
// scratch and its rows see the future — so it takes a type of its own, and
// those algorithms cannot name it by accident. The taxonomy stays closed over
// STATE SEMANTICS, not model family; `model.pass-kind()` selects it.
//
// THE CANVAS IS THE GUEST'S. The host owns the two attention modes and the
// KV; what the canvas holds (its token ids, the argmax history a stopping
// rule watches, the self-conditioning signal the next step reads) is channel
// state the guest declares, feeds and reads. The sampler — which positions
// to accept, how to renoise the rest, when to stop — is an ETA epilogue
// program over `[canvas-length, vocab]` logits, not a host policy. Nothing
// in this interface names an acceptance rule, a temperature schedule or a
// stopping criterion.
//
// Legal stages: prologue, on_attn_proj, on_attn, epilogue — the attention
// interface's. Valid only when `model.pass-kind()` == diffusion.
//
// All four interfaces deliberately DUPLICATE `embed` / `readout` / `program`
// / `submit` and their geometry records rather than sharing a `pass-body`
// (D3): the host keeps one private `PassCore`, and full separation makes
// cross-kind states unrepresentable instead of runtime-guarded.

interface forward-diffusion {
    use types.{error, data};
    use channel.{channel};
    use working-set.{kv-working-set, page-span};
    use pipeline.{pipeline};
    /// The one media-span type, `forward`'s (see `forward-hybrid`).
    use forward.{media-span};

    /// Attention geometry, field for field `forward`'s. Declared separately
    /// per interface ON PURPOSE (D8/D3).
    ///
    /// On a `denoise` pass the geometry describes the canvas: `positions`
    /// are the canvas's (the prefix length onward), `w-slot`/`w-off` land
    /// its rows in the canvas pages, and `kv-len` is prefix plus canvas — the
    /// same numbers on every step, which is what lets one pass be
    /// resubmitted for the whole denoising loop. `mask`, when bound, narrows
    /// the bidirectional reading; it never has to widen anything.
    record kv-geometry {
        readable-pages: page-span,
        writable-pages: page-span,
        kv-len:      borrow<channel>,
        pages:       borrow<channel>,
        page-indptr: borrow<channel>,
        w-slot:      borrow<channel>,
        w-off:       borrow<channel>,
        positions:   borrow<channel>,
        mask:        option<borrow<channel>>,
    }

    /// Which of the model's two readings a pass runs. See the file note.
    enum mode {
        encode,
        denoise,
    }

    resource forward-pass {
        constructor();

        /// State binding. REQUIRED — `forward.attention`, unchanged.
        attention: func(
            kv:   borrow<kv-working-set>,
            geom: kv-geometry,
        ) -> result<_, error>;

        /// The reading. REQUIRED: a diffusion pass with no mode is not
        /// submittable, because neither reading is a default the host may
        /// pick for the guest. Set before `program`; a pass keeps one mode
        /// for its life, so a loop holds one encode pass and one denoise
        /// pass rather than flipping either.
        ///
        /// A `denoise` pass on an engine whose attention cannot lift its
        /// causal bound is refused by name at submit, never read causally.
        canvas: func(mode: mode) -> result<_, error>;

        /// **THE SELF-CONDITIONING SIGNAL, AS THE GUEST'S TAPS.** The
        /// reference denoiser feeds each step the previous step's
        /// distribution, as `softmax(logits / T) · E`. Whose distribution
        /// that is — which temperature, how much of the tail — is the
        /// sampler's business, so the guest hands the model the
        /// distribution's TAPS: per canvas row, `self-cond-taps` token ids
        /// and their probabilities (`model.canvas().self-cond-taps`, row
        /// major, `rows.len() == weights.len() == length * taps`). The
        /// model gathers those rows of its embedding table with those
        /// weights and runs its self-conditioning block over the sum. Zero
        /// weights are "no signal" — the reference's first step.
        ///
        /// A payload beside the ledger, like `media`: staged for the NEXT
        /// submit of this pass and consumed by it, one at a time. A denoise
        /// pass submitted with nothing staged runs with no signal. Staging
        /// twice without a submit between is refused (the first payload
        /// would be silently lost); so is staging on an `encode` pass.
        self-conditioning: func(
            rows: list<u32>,
            weights: list<f32>,
        ) -> result<_, error>;

        /// Bind embedding token ids and CSR row indptr. Both are channels.
        /// On a `denoise` pass the tokens are the canvas — random ids on the
        /// first step, whatever the guest's sampler wrote after.
        embed: func(
            tokens: borrow<channel>,
            indptr: borrow<channel>,
        ) -> result<_, error>;

        /// Bind an optional readout-index channel separately from embedding.
        /// A denoiser reads every canvas row.
        readout: func(indices: borrow<channel>) -> result<_, error>;

        /// The media payload beside the ledger, exactly as `forward.media`
        /// states it — an `encode` pass's concern, since a prompt is what
        /// carries images.
        media: func(spans: list<media-span>) -> result<_, error>;

        /// tart: run only the first `max-layers` layers and take the head
        /// there. Zero rejected; unset = full model. Per pass, so an early
        /// denoising step may run shallow and the last ones deep.
        set-max-layers: func(max-layers: u32) -> result<_, error>;

        /// Attach canonical ETA bytes and channel handles in dense declaration
        /// order. Validation uses the engine-owned ModelProfile and rejects
        /// any stage this interface does not admit.
        program: func(
            container-bytes: data,
            channels: list<borrow<channel>>,
        ) -> result<_, error>;
    }

    /// Submit ONE FRAME on `on`: exactly `model.frame-size()` ordered slots.
    /// `forward.submit`'s contract, unchanged; an encode pass and a denoise
    /// pass may share a frame.
    submit: func(
        on: borrow<pipeline>,
        slots: list<option<borrow<forward-pass>>>,
    ) -> result<_, error>;

    /// Leave the frame's wait-set on `on` until this pipeline submits again.
    /// `forward.park`'s contract, unchanged.
    park: func(on: borrow<pipeline>);
}
